WebKit: the UI process can tell the client the main frame's scroll offset.

WKPageSetScrollOffsetCallback reports `PageDidScroll`, the message the page's process sends before each
update that scrolled (WebChromeClient::scroll), with the main frame's scroll offset. The message and the
update that follows arrive in order on one connection, so a client that notes the offset at each update
(WKPageSetUpdateCallback) knows the offset its backing store's pixels were painted at. LazyWeb+ uses it to
scroll the picture it shows ahead of the page, and to know how much of that the page has since done.

